    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`. The full suite ran (the project's only test class is `AcceptanceTest`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved all date-derived owner values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting 1 July, numbered by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 e.g. Sept 2026 \u2192 FY27), always derived from the business-day-adjusted registration date.\n\n**New file**\n- `FiscalYear.java` \u2014 helper: `of(date)` (fiscal year), `yy(date)` (two-digit segment), `label(date)` (`FY<YY>`, null-safe), and `elapsed(from, to)` (whole fiscal years between two dates).\n\n**Changed**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `fiscalYear` readOnly string field (`FY<YY>`) to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it); updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to the fiscal-year basis.\n- `OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 maps `fiscalYear` from the registration date via `FiscalYear.label(...)`, so every read/response includes it.\n- `AssignMembershipNumber.java` \u2014 the `-M<YY>` segment now uses `FiscalYear.yy(...)` instead of the raw calendar year.\n- `AssignMembershipLevel.java` \u2014 tenure points now trigger on **elapsed fiscal years \u2265 1** (`FiscalYear.elapsed(...)`) rather than a 365-day window.\n\nThe behaviour change is confirmed in the audit output: an owner registered 2026-09-07 now gets `...-M27` (FY27) rather than the old calendar-year `-M26`, and the acceptance test's `fiscalYear` (`FY\\d{2}`) assertion passes.",
